integrated_rules:
Reusable decision rule (condition-to-conclusion):

1) If AI meets ALL of the following pro-benefit conditions:
- increases society’s overall well-being via everyday convenience, health, and higher living standards;
- improves education and work outcomes;
- promotes inclusion for people with disabilities;
- improves workplace safety;
AND does NOT meet ALL of the following con-harm conditions,
THEN conclude: “Overall, artificial intelligence is good for society.”

2) If AI meets ALL of the following con-harm conditions:
- undermines critical thinking skills for students and adults alike;
- is more likely to persist and be hard to remediate for racial minorities;
- combines opacity with heavy data collection to create dangerous privacy risks;
- causes AI-induced information asymmetry that harms the economic well-being of many people and businesses;
AND does NOT meet ALL of the following pro-benefit conditions,
THEN conclude: “Overall, artificial intelligence is not good for society.”

3) If AI meets ALL of the pro-benefit conditions AND ALL of the con-harm conditions, then the criteria do not by themselves determine whether the overall verdict is “good” or “not good”; require further comparative evaluation of the relative magnitude/likelihood of benefits vs. harms and whether the harms are mitigated or are hard to remediate to a comparable evidential standard.
